## 6. Rollback plan

The probe writes exactly one additive file in a non-TCB tree, and its expensive artifact
(the matrix) is separable from its cheap artifact (the report). That asymmetry sets the
rollback strategy: **the matrix is durable, the report is a recomputable projection of it.**
Most rollbacks re-emit from the retained matrix rather than re-extracting.

| id | Trigger | Rollback action | Re-entry point |
|----|---------|-----------------|----------------|
| **R1** | A G1–G5 threshold was adjusted, reinterpreted, or "clarified" in flight. | **Void the verdict.** Adjusting a pre-registered bar after seeing the data destroys the falsification (SAEOS-1 §10 D7; PAEOS Δ3). Delete the emitted report; retain the matrix; **remand to DESIGN** for re-ratification of the threshold — never edit it in place. | DESIGN → WU-0 |
| **R2** | `matrix_variant` was switched after extraction began, or applied selectively per goal. | Void the run. A variant switch made after `N` is visible is a post-hoc threshold adjustment wearing a different name (see WU-3 rationale). Discard the matrix — it is variant-contaminated — and restart at WU-3 with the variant re-declared. | WU-3 |
| **R3** | The report was emitted from a matrix with any `UNSEEN` cell. | **Delete the report immediately.** An incomplete matrix is not a low score; it is not a result (DESIGN §4.1), and the shape-only harness would pass it. Complete the outstanding cells, then re-emit. | WU-4 |
| **R4** | The §4.2 ordering was violated — search ran before the exclusion tests, so ceremony-pruned fields were scored `absent`. | Re-adjudicate only the affected cells (a triage decision was mis-scored as evidence). Recompute `Metrics` and the verdict — both are pure over the matrix, so no re-extraction of unaffected cells is needed. Re-emit. | WU-4 (affected cells) → WU-5 |
| **R5** | A file already exists at `reviews/spikes/sakg-s1-extraction-probe.md`. | **Do not overwrite.** Inspect it first; if it is a prior probe run, preserve it (rename or record its digest) before writing. Silently replacing a prior falsification record destroys evidence. | WU-7 |
| **R6** | An acceptance assertion fails at WU-8. | Diagnose against the WU-7 emission invariants — the overwhelmingly likely cause is an unindented `verdict:`/`loss_ratio:` repetition matching at column 0, or `loss_ratio` rendered `0.4` instead of `0.40`. Fix the **rendering only**; the verdict and metrics are unchanged and MUST NOT be touched to satisfy a regex. Re-emit and re-run. | WU-7 |
| **R7** | Post-hoc review falsifies **A5** (a missing content field moves `loss_ratio` by > 0.05). | Remand to DESIGN to re-freeze §2.2, then re-run from WU-3. The prior report is retained, not deleted, and superseded explicitly — a falsified instrument is itself a finding. | DESIGN → WU-3 |

**Full-abort path.** If the probe is abandoned mid-extraction: delete any partial report and
**leave `reviews/spikes/` without the target file.** Do not emit a partial-matrix report to
"show progress" — the harness is a shape check and would pass it, converting an abandoned
run into an apparently-completed one. Absence is honest; a passing shell over an incomplete
matrix is not.

**Blast containment.** The probe touches no TCB path, no `constitution/` tree, no runtime
behaviour, and no schema. Rollback of *any* trigger above reduces to deleting or re-emitting
one additive file under `reviews/spikes/`, leaves no partial state anywhere else, and
requires no kernel amendment and no ratifier action. `reviews/tasks/` is opened read-only
throughout and is never modified by any work unit.

**What a NO-GO is *not*.** A NO-GO verdict is **not** a rollback trigger. It is the probe
working. Its consequence is the DESIGN §4.1 remand — to SAEOS-1 §6.1b / §8, with A8's
carried risk recorded as realised — and the report is retained as the evidence of that
finding, not reverted.

